  "final_brief": "# Last Night in the NBA — 2025-06-23\n\n## Quick Hits\n- Thunder defense stifles Pacers in 103-91 victory.\n- Shai Gilgeous-Alexander posts triple-double despite poor shooting.\n- Chet Holmgren anchors defense with five blocks.\n\n## Box Score Recap\nThe Oklahoma City Thunder secured a 103-91 win over the Indiana Pacers in a game defined by defensive intensity [3][4]. Despite an inefficient shooting night from Shai Gilgeous-Alexander (8-of-27 FG), the Thunder controlled the game through strong defense and timely playmaking. The Pacers were held to just 91 points, with Bennedict Mathurin leading Indiana with 24 points but struggling from the field (6-of-14) [3][6].\n\n## Standout Statlines\n- Shai Gilgeous-Alexander (Thunder): 29 points on 8-of-27 shooting, 12 assists, 5 rebounds, 2 blocks\n- Chet Holmgren (Thunder): 18 points on 6-of-8 shooting, 8 rebounds, 5 blocks\n- Bennedict Mathurin (Pacers): 24 points on 6-of-14 shooting, 13 rebounds (7 offensive)\n- T.J. McConnell (Pacers): 16 points on 8-of-13 shooting, 6 rebounds, 7 turnovers\n\n## Trades, Rumors & News\nNo notable items today.\n\n## Injuries Watch\nNo notable items today.\n\n## Storyline of the Night\nOklahoma City's 103-91 victory over Indiana was a masterclass in defensive execution, overcoming a poor shooting night from Shai Gilgeous-Alexander [3][6]. Chet Holmgren was the defensive anchor with five blocks, while Alex Caruso provided disruptive energy off the bench. The Pacers were held to 91 points, with Bennedict Mathurin's 24 points coming on inefficient shooting as Indiana's offense never found a rhythm [3][6].\n\nShai Gilgeous-Alexander posted 29 points and 12 assists despite an uncharacteristically poor 8-for-27 shooting performance [3][6]. His impact transcended scoring, as he still managed a +11 plus/minus and led his team to a double-digit win. This stat line underscores his value as a floor general who can control a game even when his shot isn't falling [3][6].\n\n## Fan Pulse\nNo notable items today.",
